408-## 7. Market instances and randomness
409-
410-### 7.1 Generator (a synthetic neighbourhood of the headline market)
411-
412-`instance(stream) -> MarketConfig` with fixed N = 10, J = 50, T = 200, sigma = 0.05, eta = 0, beta = 0.05, rho = 0.2,
413-v_min = 0.6, and per draw: q_max = 10 sorted draws from Uniform[0.55, 1.0]; b_lo ~ U[0.18, 0.26], b_hi ~ U[0.34, 0.46],
414-b_i = b_hi - (b_hi - b_lo) * rank_i / 9 + U[-0.02, 0.02] (rank by q_max, ascending; the negative quality-cost association
415-of the headline market); a_i ~ U[0, 0.05]; k_i uniform in {4, ..., 8}; v_max ~ U[1.2, 1.6]. Draws repeat inside the same
416-stream until `MarketConfig.validate()` passes (at most 10,000; the count is recorded). Calibration per instance with the
417-frozen calibrate on seeds 4000 to 4019. The claim population is this neighbourhood: fixed scale, noise, budget ratio,
418-audit cost, and cost-quality association.
419-
420-### 7.2 Streams
421-
422-- Digest: d(root, domain, n) = SHA-256 of the ASCII bytes of root + "|touchstone-v2|" + domain + "|" + decimal(n).
423-- Instance stream i: `numpy.random.Generator(numpy.random.PCG64(int.from_bytes(d(root, "instance", i)[:16], "big")))`.
424-- Development root: the string "dev-20261022"; `dev0`, `dev1` = instance streams 0 and 1. Public now.
425-- Fresh root: the block hash at height H (7.3) as 64 lowercase hex characters in the usual big-endian display order;
426-  fresh instances = streams 0 to 5, labelled `fresh0` to `fresh5`. If a stream hits the 10,000-draw limit, it is replaced
427-  by the next stream index (6, 7, ...), in order, keeping the labels in order.
428-- Evaluation seeds for market label m in {`headline`, `fresh0`, ..., `fresh5`}: v_n = 30,000,000 +
429-  int.from_bytes(d(root, "seeds/" + m, n)[:8], "big") mod 10,000,000 for n = 0, 1, ...; duplicates skipped; the first 200.
430-  The range [30,000,000, 40,000,000) is disjoint from every reserved range (`sim/amendment.py`, the amendment-1 block
431-  ending at 9,000,000, the final-evaluation range) and from the new coverage range (9.5), checked by a test.
432-- Identity salt: d(root, "salt", 0) in hex. It is public after the draw; rules never receive the salt or entity ids
433-  (spec 4.0), which a test enforces.
--
524-- **Evaluation cap 40 USD.** If the recomputed evaluation cost exceeds it, cuts apply in this order before the lock: (1)
525-  whole-system cells on fresh markets (keep headline); (2) H4 on fresh markets (Q3's independently authored part is then
526-  headline only, stated in the protocol, 8.1a); (3) D2 matched extras on fresh markets; (4) C*/S0 cells on fresh markets.
527-  If it still exceeds the cap, work stops before the lock and the author decides.
528-- **Execution priority.** Q4 and Q1 cells first, then Q2, then Q3, then the rest. If a cap is hit during a run, the
529:  remaining cells are reported incomplete, no cell is replaced, and a question whose required cells are incomplete is
530-  reported unanswered.
531-
532:## 9. Protocol v2: estimands and decision rules
533-
534-### 9.1 Estimands
535-
536-For a cell: the expected gain of the frozen attack (selected on dev-train) against the frozen reference, over the seed
537-distribution of that market, conditional on the frozen searches. Contrasts between rules use per-seed differences on
538-common seeds (the same seeds, salt, and job values for every rule in a market).
539-
540-### 9.2 Intervals and tests
541-
542-- **Bootstrap.** Studentized (percentile-t) bootstrap over seeds, 9,999 resamples, resample seed fixed in the protocol;
543-  `sim/intervals.py` extended to arbitrary tail levels. Before calibration, the smallest confirmatory tail (0.05/6, Q4)
544-  contains about 83 of the 9,999 resamples; with kappa = 1/4 it contains about 21. This is a precision limit; validation
545-  (9.5) tests the calibrated procedure itself.
546-- **p-values.** For a one-sided hypothesis about a mean, the bootstrap p-value is the smallest tail level at which the
547-  studentized one-sided bound crosses the null value; two-sided p = 2 min(one-sided p's).
548-- **Calibrated p-values.** Each confirmatory family F has one multiplier kappa_F in {1, 1/2, 1/4}, frozen by 9.5 before
549-  the lock. The calibrated p-value is p' = min(1, p / kappa_F) (one-sided), and for a two-sided test
550-  p' = min(1, 2 min(p_lower, p_upper) / kappa_F). The map is the same for every test of the family, so Holm's ordering
551-  and nesting are preserved.
552-- **Decisions** use Holm's step-down procedure on the calibrated p-values within each confirmatory family. **Reported
553-  intervals** for confirmatory families are Bonferroni simultaneous intervals at the calibrated nominal level (tail
554-  kappa_F times the Bonferroni tail); they are a separate procedure and can contain the null despite a Holm rejection.
555-  Descriptive intervals are nominal 95 percent, labelled unadjusted.
556-- **Pooled mean over the six fresh markets.** The equal-weight mean of the market means; stratified bootstrap (seeds
557-  resampled independently within each market, since seed streams are independent across markets), studentized by the
558-  stratified standard error; conditional on the six markets.
559-- **G_min** (descriptive). Bounds as the minimum over members of the members' bounds at level 0.05 / c.
560-- **Numerical rules (as implemented in `sim/intervals.py`, frozen with the code).** A sample with a nonfinite per-seed
561-  value is refused and its cell is missing (6.5). A nonfinite standard error, bootstrap statistic, bound, or p-value is
562-  refused in the same way: the affected decision is withheld ("not decided") and the cell is missing. A constant sample (all values exactly equal) has standard error
563-  0: its bounds equal its mean, and its one-sided p-value is 0 if the mean is strictly beyond the null and 1 otherwise. A
564-  resample with zero standard error is studentized by the full-sample standard error. p-values have resolution
565-  1 / (B + 1) with B = 9,999: p = (count + 1) / (B + 1), where count is the number of resampled statistics at least as
566-  extreme (ties count as extreme), and p = 1 if every resample is at least as extreme. Quantiles use numpy's default
567:  (linear) interpolation. A search that returns no finite candidate leaves its cell incomplete.
568-
569-### 9.3 Decision rules
570-
571-- **Q1, confirmatory on the pooled fresh markets.** Family of 2 tests: F111 vs C*, families D2+H3 and X7-SC, each on
572-  the pooled mean d = g_C* - g_F111 of the per-seed differences over the six fresh markets. Statistical direction and
573-  practical size are reported separately. Holm on the calibrated two-sided p-values for H0: d = 0 (one-sided tails
574-  0.05/4, 0.05/2) decides the direction; the calibrated Bonferroni interval I (two-sided 0.05/2 times kappa_Q1) decides
575-  the size against the margin 0.05. Exclusive categories, in order:
576-  1. **materially lower**: I lies entirely above 0.05 ("R3 materially lower gain") or entirely below -0.05 ("C*
577-     materially lower gain");
578-  2. **equivalent**: I lies inside [-0.05, 0.05] (a conservative two one-sided tests procedure); if Holm also rejects
579-     zero, the label is "equivalent, direction R3 (or C*) lower";
580-  3. **ordered, size not established**: Holm rejects zero, and neither category 1 nor category 2 applies;
581-  4. **inconclusive**.
582-  The margin 0.05 is half the registered comparative margin 0.10 and below the headline B3 - R3 difference (0.28).
583-  **Efficiency condition.** The word "better" for a rule needs category 1 in its favour and, for the same family, the
584-  pooled paired gross-efficiency difference (that rule minus the other) with a nominal one-sided 97.5 percent lower bound
585-  of at least -0.01 (descriptive, not calibrated). Any combined "better" statement says that its efficiency part is
586-  descriptive evidence without a calibrated joint error guarantee. If the lower bound is below -0.01, the result is
587-  reported by its gain category with "efficiency non-inferiority unresolved"; only if the nominal one-sided 97.5 percent
588-  upper bound is below -0.01 is it reported as "lower gain with an efficiency loss" (descriptive). Beside every Q1 result: paired gross and net efficiency, the truthful
589-  reference profit, audit spending, refunds from false failures of the focal and the other providers, and the served
590-  share. Per-market and headline Q1 results are descriptive (the headline market was used in development).
591-- **Q2, descriptive.** Per market and family, effect coding over the 8 cells: main effect of P = mean of the 4 cells with
592-  P = 1 minus the mean of the 4 with P = 0 (likewise G, S); two-way interaction PG = (mean of cells with P = G) minus
593-  (mean with P != G), likewise PS, GS; three-way PGS = (mean of cells with an odd number of 1s) minus (mean with an even
594-  number); conditional effects G at P = 0 and P = 1, P at S = 0 and S = 1, P at G = 0 and G = 1, and G at S = 0 and
595-  S = 1. Both versions: separately searched attacks (diagonal) and fixed attacks (6.6, with the attacker-profit and
596-  reference terms and efficiency beside each contrast). Pooled over the 6 fresh markets as in 9.2. Nominal 95 percent
597-  intervals, no decisions. Every factorial cell uses the fixed pair; no factorial cell is tuned separately.
598-- **Q3, descriptive.** Per core rule (F000, F111, C*), the attack envelope: the family among {D2+H3, X7-SC, X5-AG/v2,
599-  H4} with the highest training gain, evaluated unchanged; the training-selected lowest efficiency under W0 and W1 (6.2)
600:  with the W1 classification; coalition G_tot and G_min for X6-SE/v2 and X8-DB; the diagnostic (6.7). An incomplete
601-  scheduled check makes that rule's statement for that family unanswered (6.5).
602-- **Q4, confirmatory on sampled markets.** Per fresh market, an intersection-union test: (a) H0: g_F000 - g_F111 <= 0.10
603-  under D2+H3 and (b) H0: F111's gross efficiency <= 0.90, each one-sided; the market's p-value is the larger of the two
604-  calibrated component p-values. A market passes if Holm across the 6 market p-values rejects it (one-sided tails 0.05/6 down to
605-  0.05/1). Reported: k of 6 markets passing, and the markets. Wording: "replicated on k of 6 sampled markets"; failing
606-  markets are named; "generalisation not established" when a market is not decided. No population claim.
607-
608-### 9.4 Claim scope
609-
610-All claims are conditional on the six sampled markets, the stated neighbourhood, the fixed pair (Q1, Q2, Q4), the searched
611-families, and the frozen searches. Q4 replicates the B3 - R3 comparison under D2+H3 and R3's efficiency condition, not the
612-complete amended PASS predicate of the first evaluation. Multiplicity is controlled within Q1 and within Q4 separately;
613-there is no paper-wide 5 percent familywise guarantee across all confirmatory statements, and the paper does not claim
614-one. "Confirmatory" means specified before the fresh draw; it does not mean exact nominal error control on the fresh,
615-selected-policy distributions (9.5). Q4 is an internal replication on fresh instances, not an independent-team
616-replication.
617-
618-### 9.5 Coverage check and calibration (before the lock)
619-
620-- **Fixtures.** On the headline market, `dev0`, and `dev1`, frozen development attacks (the inner searches at the fixed
621-  pair from tuning and comparator selection) and development references (6.4) are run on pools of 20,000 seeds per market
622-  in the new range [40,000,000, 60,000,000). Profiles per seed (8): F000's D2+H3 attack (its X7-SC attack is not needed
623-  for any confirmatory statistic and is omitted), the D2+H3 and X7-SC attacks of F111 and C*, and the three rules'
624-  references (one reference profile per rule; the two references of a rule's families are the same frozen reference, 6.4,
625-  so a family-specific reference profile is not needed). Every profile of a seed uses that seed's common random numbers,
626-  so per-seed differences keep their dependence.
627-- **Q1 fixture (pooled).** Six strata: each of the three markets split into two disjoint pools of 10,000. A replication
628-  draws 200 seeds with replacement from each stratum, independently across strata and replications, and computes the
629-  pooled statistic and its bounds; the truth is the equal-weight mean of the six stratum pool means.
630-- **Q4 fixtures (per market).** A replication draws 200 seeds with replacement from the market's 20,000-seed pool; the
631-  truth is the pool mean. The multiplier must pass on all three market fixtures and is then applied to the six fresh
632-  markets (a stated transfer assumption).
633-- **Endpoints.** Q1: both sides of the two pooled differences at the Holm tails 0.05/4 and 0.05/2 (8 endpoints; the
634-  Bonferroni interval of 9.3 uses tail 0.05/4, which is among them). Q4: the one-sided lower bounds of the gain difference
635-  and of the efficiency at tails 0.05/6, 0.05/5, ..., 0.05/1 on each of the three markets (36 endpoints). For an endpoint
636-  with allocated tail alpha_e, a candidate kappa uses the bound at nominal tail kappa alpha_e.
637-- **Selection.** On 10,000 selection replications per fixture, kappa_F is the largest of 1, 1/2, 1/4 for which every
638-  endpoint of family F, on every fixture, has a Clopper-Pearson upper bound of its miss rate, at confidence
639-  1 - 0.01 / m_F, at most alpha_e (the validation criterion applied to the selection replications), so that a candidate
640-  with near-nominal size is not selected only to fail validation.
641-- **Validation.** On 20,000 independent validation replications per fixture (new resampling seeds), the selected kappa_F
642-  is admitted if, for every endpoint of the family, the Clopper-Pearson upper bound of the miss rate at confidence
643-  1 - 0.01 / m_F is at most alpha_e (m_F = 8 for Q1, 36 for Q4; Bonferroni over the family's endpoints, so the
644-  guarantee holds jointly with Monte Carlo confidence at least 99 percent). Only the selected procedure is validated, so
645-  the selection does not affect this guarantee. There is no fallback to another candidate after a failed validation.
646-- **Failure.** If no candidate passes selection, or the selected one fails validation, the family receives no
647-  confirmatory decision (estimates and descriptive intervals are still reported).
648-- **Fixture branches (registered for every development-cut case of 8.2).** Seeds of a market's coverage pool are taken
649-  in the order of their derivation index; stratum s of a market takes the s-th consecutive block of floor(M / k) seeds,
650-  where M is the market's pool size and k the number of strata per market; the remainder is discarded for Q1 only. Q1
651-  always has six equal-weight strata, sampled with replacement:
652-  - three markets, M = 20,000: k = 2 strata of 10,000 per market (the default);
653-  - two markets (headline, `dev0`), M = 20,000: k = 3 strata of 6,666 per market;
654-  - two markets (headline, `dev0`), M = 10,000: k = 3 strata of 3,333 per market (8 profiles x 10,000 x 2 = 160,000
655-    profile episodes, about 9 units, plus bootstrap work).
--
673-accrue; R4 removes the rho = 1 collapse; Q4: k >= 5. Final predictions, including X8-DB and H4, are written from
674-development results before the lock.
