cost cap once after 11 of its 12 cells had been seen.

Failure modes. Exploratory robustness cells on reused development seeds found three: at high
audit cost (ρ = 1) R3’s terminal reserve consumes the whole allowance and R3 audits nothing early;
high noise lowers every audited rule’s efficiency; and coalitions of entrants overload the newcomer
priority. The full timeline and the robustness table are in the supplement; the record and its claims
are unchanged.


8    The second test: design and registration
Questions. Q1: does R3 (F111 ) leave a lower or higher identity-replacement gain than C∗ , at the
fixed pair? Q2: what do the priority phase, the residual targeting, and the pacing contribute, alone
and together? Q3: do the results hold against X7-SC, X5-AG/v2, the defence-aware sealed family
H4, welfare attacks, and coalitions, and does R4 repair the reserve collapse? Q4: does the first test’s
comparison of B3 and R3 under D2+H3 replicate on fresh markets? Q1 and Q4 are confirmatory;
Q2 and Q3 are descriptive.

Development. All choices before the draw were development, on the headline market and the
development instance dev0 (branch two20k): a benchmark priced every cell; the comparator was
selected and the tuned variants were tuned; the defenders were frozen at a signed commit; a separate
session wrote H4 against the frozen defenders and committed only its hash; a coverage check on
frozen development attacks admitted calibration multipliers κ = 0.5 for Q1 and κ = 0.25 for Q4;
and the predictions were written and hashed. Budget caps were 12 USD for development and 40
USD for evaluation, within a written authorisation of 52 USD. The evaluation cap forced three
registered cuts: the tuned variants, H4, and the D2 extras run on the headline market only.



                                                  9
Lock and draw. The protocol file binds the code commit, the rules, families, tuned files, man-
ifest, decision rules, predictions, budget, and calibration multipliers. It was signed and tagged
protocol-v2, stamped with OpenTimestamps, and pushed with its upgraded proof when the Bit-
coin tip was at block 974379. The earliest attestation of the proof is at block A = 974307; the
draw block is H = A + 144 = 974451, read once both explorers reported at least six blocks above
it with the same hash. Had the push come at or after H, the draw would have been kept and its
results labelled nonconfirmatory. We assume that the chain both explorers served at that read is
the accepted chain and that no miner chose the block at H to influence this evaluation; a later
reorganisation deeper than six blocks is not handled and would be reported.

Fresh markets. The block hash seeds a generator of market instances around the headline market:
fixed scale, noise, budget ratio, audit cost, and cost-quality association, with provider ceilings, costs,
capacities, and the top job value drawn per instance (Appendix A). Six instances and 200 evaluation
seeds per market were drawn from it. The claims concern this neighbourhood, not markets in
general.

Decision rules. Table 3 summarises them; the protocol file is the authority. Pooled statistics
weight the six markets equally, and intervals use a studentized bootstrap (9999 resamples) that
resamples seeds within each market, pairing the rules’ outcomes on each seed; this measures un-
certainty over seeds in the six fixed markets, not over new markets or repeated searches. The gain
is g = (π A − π T )/(S ∗ /N ), attacked profit minus the profit of the rule’s frozen truthful reference,
normalised by the first-best surplus per provider; with N = 10 providers, the Q1 margin of 0.05 is
half a percent of the first-best surplus. Calibration. A multiplier κ replaces each nominal tail αe by
καe , so κ < 1 widens the intervals. It was selected from {1, 1/2, 1/4} on fixtures built from frozen
development attacks and references on the headline market and dev0 (pools of 20000 seeds per mar-
ket; Q1 in six equal strata), using 10000 selection replications, and admitted on 20000 independent
validation replications if every endpoint’s Clopper–Pearson upper bound on its miss rate, at confi-
dence 1−0.01/m with m = 8 endpoints for Q1 and m = 36 for Q4 (the two-market branch evaluates
24 Q4 endpoints and keeps the denominator 36, which is conservative), was at most its allocated
tail. The fixtures hold the policies and references fixed, so admission does not cover search or refer-
ence selection, and it does not establish nominal coverage on the fresh, selected-rule distributions.
Q1’s categories follow the logic of equivalence testing and Q4’s market test is an intersection-union
test [4]; the calibration follows the reporting of simulation studies of statistical methods [19]. Con-
ditional on valid calibrated component p-values, Holm controls the Q1 direction family and the six
Q4 joint gain-and-efficiency decisions separately; fixture admission does not establish that validity
on the fresh-market distributions. Q1’s reported intervals use calibrated Bonferroni tails, and its
efficiency condition uses a separate nominal one-sided 97.5 percent bound. The report stores the
calibrated two-sided Q1 value that enters Holm and, for Q4, the raw one-sided bootstrap p-values of
the two components and the calibrated market value max(p′gain , p′eff ) with p′ = min(1, p/κ); Tables 5
and 8 print these with their Holm-adjusted values, and an audit script recomputes these values from
the records and reproduces each Q1 category and direction rejection and each Q4 decision.
     Q4 replicates the B3–R3 comparison and R3’s efficiency condition, not the complete amended
claim of the first test. The two questions have separate multiplicity families; no paper-wide five
percent guarantee is claimed. “Confirmatory” means specified before the draw; coverage admission
rests on development fixtures and does not establish nominal coverage on the fresh, selected-rule
distributions. Q4 is an internal replication, not an independent team’s.



