and population profitability remains unestablished. The evaluation-selected weights are the largest
at which a nominal one-sided 95 percent normal bound on the mean gain stays non-negative (the
training-feasible row also requires a non-negative training mean); that bound is not adjusted for
selection, the weight and the companion family were chosen on the evaluation seeds, and the bound
is close to zero at the selected weights, so this checks the policy class and is not an out-of-sample
estimate. The mixture efficiencies stay above 0.90; the analysis does not touch Q4 or its registered
predicate. For coalitions of two synchronised members, the selected total gains are 0.584 against B3
and 0.058 against R3 (search flagged); for five, 0.191 and 0.108 (total objective), while the policy
selected for the minimum-member objective leaves R3’s worst member a mean gain of −0.001;
the decoy-and-beneficiary family gives 0.024 against B3 and 0.025 against R3. These comparisons
establish neither coalition stability nor an ordering across every coalition family (Table 15). At
ρ = 1, where R3’s terminal reserve collapses, R3 leaves 0.565 under D2+H3; the repair R4 lowers
this to 0.356 and C∗ with the capped schedule (C∗ /S2) to 0.434 (Table 16).

Secondary comparison. At the headline market, with each rule’s own tuned parameters, the
gains are 0.356 (B3-t), 0.060 (R3-t), and 0.082 (C∗ -t) under D2+H3. This comparison is secondary
and descriptive.

