registered scoring rule also says that an incomplete target, in the sense of the design’s search-quality
checks, is not assessed; those checks call a search-flagged cell incomplete while keeping its estimate
for the prescribed search procedure; it does not say whether a prediction computed from a flagged
search is itself incomplete. Table 21 therefore adds two stricter readings, computed from a map
of the searches each prediction cell requires (released with the scorer). If a flagged required search
makes a Q3 policy comparison ineligible, Q3-a, Q3-b, and Q3-d are not assessed: 7 predictions are
fulfilled, 8 fail, and 3 are not assessed. If it makes every descriptive prediction ineligible (all except
those that score a confirmatory decision: Q1-a, its alternative, Q1-b, Q1-d, Q4-a, and Q4-b), Q1-c
and Q2-a to Q2-c are also not assessed: 6, 5, and 7. No reading turns a failed prediction into
a fulfilled one or changes a registered decision; the marked rows of Tables 19 and 20 are those a
stricter reading changes, and their estimates stand as estimates of the prescribed searches. The
predictions differ in stakes and are not independent, so the count of fulfilled predictions is not a
validation score. The failures are informative: we expected the priority phase to matter little, the
replication to hold on at least five markets, X5-AG/v2 to stay below D2+H3, and the unconstrained
welfare attack to leave R3’s and C∗ ’s efficiency at the registered level, and none of these held (the
first is not assessed under the descriptive reading).




