\paragraph{Predictions.} Tables~\ref{tab:predictions} and~\ref{tab:predictions-b} score every registered prediction from the report. For Q4-b, the original scoring script compared the report's raw component $p$-values, although the prediction is registered on calibrated values; the corrected script compares the calibrated values, \site{r_pred_q4b_g} against \site{r_pred_q4b_e} on each of fresh0, fresh2, and fresh5. The prediction remains fulfilled and the total is unchanged: a scoring correction with no change of verdict, separate from the corrected labels of Tables~\ref{tab:q1} and~\ref{tab:q4}. The scoring script reads the numerical estimates only: it retains the estimates of completed searches whose doubling check was marked incomplete, and counts \site{r_pred_n_ful} predictions fulfilled and \site{r_pred_n_fail} failed. The registered scoring rule also says that an incomplete target, in the sense of the design's search-quality checks, is not assessed; those checks call a search-flagged cell incomplete while keeping its estimate for the prescribed search procedure; it does not say whether a prediction computed from a flagged search is itself incomplete. Table~\ref{tab:pred-eligibility} therefore adds two stricter readings, computed from a map of the searches each prediction cell requires (released with the scorer). If a flagged required search makes a Q3 policy comparison ineligible, Q3-a, Q3-b, and Q3-d are not assessed: \site{r_pred_q_ful} predictions are fulfilled, \site{r_pred_q_fail} fail, and \site{r_pred_q_na} are not assessed. If it makes every descriptive prediction ineligible (all except those that score a confirmatory decision: Q1-a, its alternative, Q1-b, Q1-d, Q4-a, and Q4-b), Q1-c and Q2-a to Q2-c are also not assessed: \site{r_pred_d_ful}, \site{r_pred_d_fail}, and \site{r_pred_d_na}. No reading turns a failed prediction into a fulfilled one or changes a registered decision; the marked rows of Tables~\ref{tab:predictions} and~\ref{tab:predictions-b} are those a stricter reading changes, and their estimates stand as estimates of the prescribed searches. The predictions differ in stakes and are not independent, so the count of fulfilled predictions is not a validation score. The failures are informative: we expected the priority phase to matter little, the replication to hold on at least five markets, X5-AG/v2 to stay below D2+H3, and the unconstrained welfare attack to leave R3's and C$^*$'s efficiency at the registered level, and none of these held (the first is not assessed under the descriptive reading).

